    topic: "Pressure-testing the forecast",
    level: 3,
    type: "qa",
    q: "In late September, the operator forecasts $4.8M of fourth-quarter rooms revenue. How do you pressure-test it?",
    a: "Rebuild it from what's already booked. Take what's on the books for the quarter and add the pickup the hotel has historically achieved from the same date, then compare the total with the forecast. Test group and transient separately, check the rate on new bookings and ask what specific business closes any gap.",
    why: "Hotels book forward: groups months or years out, transient mostly in the final weeks. So a quarterly forecast has two parts: business on the books, which is largely known, and pickup still to come, which is judgment. Pace, on-the-books business against the same date last year, ties the pickup assumption to the hotel's own history. Group and transient pick up at different speeds, so test them apart. The comp set's forward-looking data shows whether the whole market is pacing ahead or behind. A forecast well above pace needs named business behind it.",
    formula: "Implied final room nights = room nights on the books ÷ share usually booked by this date\nPace-based revenue = revenue on the books + pickup room nights × pickup ADR",
    example: "On the books: [[13,200]] room nights at a [[$190]] ADR, $2.51M. Last year at this date, [[55%]] of the quarter's final room nights were booked, implying 24,000 in all. The other 10,800 at [[$185]] add $2.00M, for about $4.51M. The operator's [[$4.8M]] is $0.29M, about 6.5%, above what the hotel's own pace supports.",
    trap: "Last year's booking curve isn't always the right guide: a holiday shift, a citywide event or a change in group mix alters it. Track the operator's forecast accuracy too; a consistent bias tells you how much to haircut the next forecast."
