    q: "How do you benchmark a hotel's operating costs against its peers?",
    a: "Build a peer set of similar hotels (chain scale, service level, size and location type) and compare each cost line on the right basis: per occupied room for costs driven by stays, per available room for fixed overhead and percent of revenue for costs driven by sales. Convert each gap into dollars, then ask the operator to explain the biggest.",
    why: "A budget variance shows whether the hotel hit its own plan; benchmarking shows whether the plan was any good. Peer data comes from anonymized USALI-format P&Ls collected by benchmarking services, the owner's other hotels and the operator's portfolio. Because USALI defines each line the same way, a persistent gap usually points to something real: overstaffing, costly contracts, weak purchasing or heavy centralized charges. Some gaps are legitimate, though: union wage scales, local utility rates and an older building raise costs that no operator can fix.",
    example: "A [[250]]-key hotel spends [[$4,800]] per available room a year on administrative and general costs against a peer median of [[$4,000]]: $200K. Rooms costs run [[$52]] per occupied room against [[$47]], on [[65,000]] occupied rooms: $325K. Closing half of both gaps adds about $263K of GOP, worth about $3.3M at an [[8%]] cap rate before any incentive fee.",
    trap: "The follow-up: what if the peers have a different revenue mix? A hotel with more F&B shows a lower margin even if every department is run as well as the peers', so adjust for mix before judging the operator."
