    topic: "What to benchmark against",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What does an asset manager benchmark a hotel against, and what does each comparison catch?",
    a: "Four things. The comp set, for revenue share: RGI and its occupancy and rate parts. A peer set of similar hotels' P&Ls, for costs and margins. The hotel's own budget and history, for drift and trends. And guest scores and brand quality audits, for the product and service. Each catches what the others miss.",
    why: "Each benchmark can mislead on its own. A hotel can grow RGI by discounting or buying business through costly channels, which the P&L comparison exposes. It can beat peers on cost by cutting service or maintenance, which guest scores and audits reveal before revenue suffers. And it can beat a cautious budget while lagging its peers. Guest scores matter financially because they drive online rankings and pricing power, and repeated failed quality audits can put a franchised hotel in default.",
    trap: "Watch the benchmarks themselves. Operators can propose comp set changes that flatter RGI, and peer sets drift, so many owners require approval to change the comp set and record why each hotel is in it.",
