    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "You've just bought a hotel and kept its operator. Where do you look for NOI upside?",
    a: "Revenue first: pricing and segment mix, cheaper booking channels, fees the market will accept and underused space, such as a restaurant to lease or convert. Then costs: labor productivity, purchasing, energy, service contracts and centralized charges. Then below GOP: property tax appeals and insurance. Finally the contracts: brand costs and fees at renewal. Rank ideas by value, speed and risk.",
    why: "The levers differ in size, speed and risk. Revenue levers are often the largest because higher rates flow almost straight to profit, but they depend on the market and take time to prove. Cost levers are quicker and mostly within the operator's control, though cuts guests notice can cost revenue later. Below-GOP items such as a tax appeal need no operational change at all. Capitalized, each dollar of NOI adds many dollars of value, so small items add up.",
    example: "At a [[250]]-key hotel: leasing an underused restaurant to an outside operator adds [[$250K]], shifting bookings from online agencies to direct channels saves [[$120K]], a property tax appeal saves [[$180K]] and energy projects save [[$90K]]. Together that's $640K of NOI, worth $8.0M at an [[8%]] cap rate.",
    trap: "Check the contracts before promising anything. The management agreement usually gives the operator control of pricing and staffing, and the franchise fixes service standards, so many levers need the operator's or brand's agreement."
