    q: "Your operator looks likely to fail its performance test this year. What do you do in practice?",
    a: "Prepare before year-end. Confirm the exact terms: test years, thresholds, exclusions, notice windows and cure rights. Track both prongs monthly and check the comp set hasn't changed. Document that the owner funded everything it owed, since underfunding is the operator's best defense. Then decide the goal: termination, or leverage to renegotiate fees, the general manager or the budget.",
    why: "Performance tests are hard to use: they usually need two bad years, exclude disrupted periods, allow cures and turn on definitions the operator helped set. So enforcement starts long before the test date. The budget you approve becomes the GOP baseline and the comp set you accept becomes the index baseline, so approve both with the test in mind. When failure looms, the test is often worth more as leverage than as an exit: an operator facing termination will often cut fees, replace the general manager or accept a tougher test to keep the contract.",
