    q: "The operator proposes cutting ADR from $200 to $180, expecting occupancy to rise from 70% to 79%. Each occupied room costs $40 to service. Would you agree?",
    a: "Not on the rooms alone. RevPAR rises from $140.00 to $142.20, so the operator's and brand's revenue-based fees grow, but room profit falls from $112.00 to $110.60 per available room night. The cut pays only if each extra occupied room brings more than about $16 of profit from F&B and other spending, and only if competitors don't match it.",
    why: "The owner and operator see the same proposal differently. The operator's base fee and the brand's royalty grow with revenue, and higher RevPAR lifts the index the operator is judged on. The owner keeps the profit, which depends on the margin on every room, not just the number of rooms sold. Ancillary spending is what can make occupancy worth buying, which is why resorts and group hotels price for occupancy more readily than select-service hotels with little F&B.",
    example: "Per available room night: today [[70%]] × ([[$200]] − [[$40]]) = $112.00; after the cut, [[79%]] × ($180 − $40) = $110.60. The extra 9 points of occupancy must make up $1.40, so each extra occupied room needs $1.40 ÷ 9% = $15.56 of other profit. A resort with busy restaurants clears that easily; a select-service hotel may not.",
    trap: "Ask how the discount is given. A targeted rate, such as an advance-purchase or package rate, can win new guests without cutting the price for those who'd have paid $200 anyway; a cut to the public rate gives it away to everyone."
