    topic: "Pricing in a downturn",
    level: 2,
    type: "qa",
    classic: true,
    q: "Demand in your market falls sharply, and the operator wants to cut rates to hold occupancy. What's your view as the owner?",
    a: "Be skeptical of broad rate cuts. In a downturn, lower prices create little new travel, competitors match within days and the market ends up with the same guests at lower rates, which take years to rebuild. Prefer targeted offers such as advance-purchase rates and packages, base business like groups and contracts, stronger sales effort and cost flex, while watching RGI.",
    why: "Most hotel demand comes from reasons to travel, such as business, events and leisure plans, so market-wide demand responds little to price: a cheaper room rarely creates a trip. One hotel can win share by cutting rate, but competitors see it quickly and follow, so the gain fades while the lower rate becomes the market's reference point, including for corporate and group rates negotiated for the next year. Targeted discounts keep the public rate intact, and holding rate positions the hotel to recover faster.",
    example: "A [[250]]-key hotel at [[65%]] occupancy and a [[$200]] ADR cuts rates [[10%]], and competitors follow, so occupancy stays at 65%. RevPAR falls from $130 to $117: $1.19M of rooms revenue a year, nearly all of it lost profit, because no extra rooms were sold and no costs were saved.",
    trap: "Don't turn this into 'never discount'. If the hotel's rate sits well above competitors' and it's losing share, or the market has collapsed and fixed costs need covering, targeted price moves can be right. Judge them on profit and RGI together."
